<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southern African Customs Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as SACU</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Southern African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">Customs Union</a>, established in 1910 with the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, is the oldest existing customs union in the world. Its members are South Africa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>, which share a common external tariff and divide the pooled customs revenue by a formula. These revenues form a large part of the budgets of the smaller members.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southern-african-customs-union.jpg" alt="Southern African Customs Union" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lokal_Profil; derivative work by Htonl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_African_Customs_Union.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> in the region was formed in 1889 between the British Cape Colony and the Orange Free State. British Bechuanaland and Basutoland joined in 1891, the Bechuanaland Protectorate in 1893 and Natal in 1899, while the South African Republic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a> formed a separate union in 1894. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> a new Convention of 1903 created the Southern African Customs Union from Cape Colony, Basutoland, the Bechuanaland Protectorate, Natal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a>, the Transvaal Colony and Southern Rhodesia. Swaziland joined in 1904 and North-western Rhodesia in 1905, and a convention of 1906 replaced the 1903 agreement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The present union was established in 1910 under a Customs Union Agreement between the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-commission-territories">High Commission Territories</a> of Bechuanaland, Basutoland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>. Southern Rhodesia and North-western Rhodesia declined to join but kept a common tariff and free trade arrangements with South Africa until 1935. With the independence of the High Commission territories the agreement was updated, and it was relaunched as the SACU on 11 December 1969, entering into force on 1 March 1970. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> joined in 1990 as the fifth member after independence from South Africa, though it had been effectively part of the union since South Africa conquered it in 1915. South Africa administered the union through the agreements of 1910 and 1969.</p><p>After the Government of National Unity took office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in April 1994, the member states agreed to renegotiate the agreement to democratise the union. Ministers reached consensus in September 2000 on a Council of Ministers, a Commission, a Tribunal and a Secretariat. In 2006 SACU concluded a free trade agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-free-trade-association">European Free Trade Association</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The union's aim is the free interchange of goods among its members. It has a common external tariff and a common excise tariff, and its headquarters are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>. All customs and excise collected in the common area are paid into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s National Revenue Fund, which holds the pool. Revenue is then shared by a formula with three parts: a customs component allocated by each country's share of intra-SACU trade, an excise component allocated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>, and a development component of 15 percent of the excise pool, distributed in inverse proportion to GDP per capita. Only the shares of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> are calculated, and South Africa keeps the residual. The revenue forms a substantial share of those four states' revenue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Southern African Customs Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today the country belongs to the <b>Southern African Customs Union</b>, and its currency, the lilangeni, is pegged to the South African rand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
